From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ahmedawachi <121810925+ahmedawachi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 09:30:51 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/8] ae_select: keep every registered fd below FD_SETSIZE

What: on builds that use the select() event loop (no epoll, kqueue or
evport; on the platforms we ship that means Cygwin and MSYS2):
- config.h defines USE_AE_SELECT for exactly the case in which ae.c
  includes ae_select.c.
- adjustOpenFilesLimit() first clamps maxclients to
  FD_SETSIZE - CONFIG_FDSET_INCR - 1 (895 with FD_SETSIZE 1024) and logs a
  WARNING naming the old and new value. The event loop is created with
  maxclients + CONFIG_FDSET_INCR, so aeCreateFileEvent() now rejects any
  fd that the fd_set cannot hold. This is the rule aeResizeSetSize()
  already applies to CONFIG SET maxclients; startup never checked it.
  Because updateMaxclients() also calls adjustOpenFilesLimit(), CONFIG SET
  maxclients above the limit is refused with "... try with 895".
- aeApiAddEvent() refuses fd >= FD_SETSIZE with ERANGE. This protects
  redis-benchmark and redis-cli, which size their loops at 10240.
- The build fails with #error if FD_SETSIZE <= CONFIG_FDSET_INCR + 1
  (newlib's FD_SETSIZE is 64 when __CYGWIN__ is not predefined), so that
  assumption can no longer fail silently.

Why: MSYS2/Cygwin cap open files at 3200, so Redis starts with maxclients 3168
and an event loop of 3296 while fd_set holds 1024 bits. The first fd of
1024 or more makes FD_SET write past rfds, and Cygwin's select() zeroes
and rewrites sizeof_fd_set(maxfd+1) bytes starting at _wfds, the last
member of the 512-byte aeApiState. That overruns the heap on every
wakeup, or panics in aeApiPoll on EBADF. Production is not exposed
today (client fds 8 and 9), but any connection burst of about 1000
reaches it.

Not done: raising FD_SETSIZE (-DFD_SETSIZE=2048). It is sound only if
every translation unit that uses fd_set sees the same value, and it buys
nothing for a two-connection cache. The clamp is written in terms of
FD_SETSIZE, so a build that passes -DFD_SETSIZE=2048 in CFLAGS (used by
both src and deps) raises the cap automatically. The runtime's NUM_SOCKS
2048 sockets-per-session limit still applies.

Risk: very low. No-op on epoll, kqueue and evport builds (ae_select.c is
not compiled there and the clamp is under USE_AE_SELECT). On select
builds, clients above 895 get "-ERR max number of clients reached"
instead of undefined behaviour.
---
 src/ae_select.c |  7 +++++++
 src/config.h    |  6 ++++++
 src/server.c    | 26 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 39 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/ae_select.c b/src/ae_select.c
index c73b875..bcc7b7a 100644
--- a/src/ae_select.c
+++ b/src/ae_select.c
@@ -43,6 +43,13 @@ static void aeApiFree(aeEventLoop *eventLoop) {
 static int aeApiAddEvent(aeEventLoop *eventLoop, int fd, int mask) {
     aeApiState *state = eventLoop->apidata;
 
+    /* FD_SET() does no bounds checking, so an fd the fd_set cannot hold
+     * would be written past it. aeCreateFileEvent() then fails without
+     * recording the fd, so it never reaches aeApiDelEvent() or select(). */
+    if (fd >= FD_SETSIZE) {
+        errno = ERANGE;
+        return -1;
+    }
     if (mask & AE_READABLE) FD_SET(fd,&state->rfds);
     if (mask & AE_WRITABLE) FD_SET(fd,&state->wfds);
     return 0;
diff --git a/src/config.h b/src/config.h
index beb6bc8..373cf54 100644
--- a/src/config.h
+++ b/src/config.h
@@ -117,6 +117,12 @@
 #endif
 #endif
 
+/* No epoll, kqueue or evport: ae.c falls back to select() (e.g. Cygwin/MSYS2),
+ * whose fd_set holds only FD_SETSIZE descriptors. */
+#if !defined(HAVE_EVPORT) && !defined(HAVE_EPOLL) && !defined(HAVE_KQUEUE)
+#define USE_AE_SELECT 1
+#endif
+
 /* Test for __builtin_prefetch()
  * Supported in LLVM since 2.9: https://releases.llvm.org/2.9/docs/ReleaseNotes.html
  * Supported in GCC since 3.1 but we use 4.8 given it's too old: https://gcc.gnu.org/gcc-3.1/changes.html. */
diff --git a/src/server.c b/src/server.c
index e8cd65f..69a0868 100644
--- a/src/server.c
+++ b/src/server.c
@@ -2685,6 +2685,29 @@ int setOOMScoreAdj(int process_class) {
 #endif
 }
 
+#ifdef USE_AE_SELECT
+#include <sys/select.h>
+#if FD_SETSIZE <= CONFIG_FDSET_INCR + 1
+#error "FD_SETSIZE is too small for the select() event loop"
+#endif
+
+/* The select() event loop cannot watch an fd >= FD_SETSIZE. Keep the event
+ * loop size (maxclients + CONFIG_FDSET_INCR) below FD_SETSIZE, the same rule
+ * aeResizeSetSize() applies to CONFIG SET maxclients, so every fd that
+ * aeCreateFileEvent() accepts also fits in the fd_set. */
+static void clampMaxclientsForSelect(void) {
+    const unsigned int limit = FD_SETSIZE - CONFIG_FDSET_INCR - 1;
+
+    if (server.maxclients > limit) {
+        serverLog(LL_WARNING,"This build uses the select() event loop, which "
+            "cannot watch file descriptors of %d or higher (FD_SETSIZE). "
+            "maxclients has been reduced from %u to %u.",
+            FD_SETSIZE, server.maxclients, limit);
+        server.maxclients = limit;
+    }
+}
+#endif
+
 /* This function will try to raise the max number of open files accordingly to
  * the configured max number of clients. It also reserves a number of file
  * descriptors (CONFIG_MIN_RESERVED_FDS) for extra operations of
@@ -2694,6 +2717,9 @@ int setOOMScoreAdj(int process_class) {
  * max number of clients, the function will do the reverse setting
  * server.maxclients to the value that we can actually handle. */
 void adjustOpenFilesLimit(void) {
+#ifdef USE_AE_SELECT
+    clampMaxclientsForSelect();
+#endif
     rlim_t maxfiles = server.maxclients+CONFIG_MIN_RESERVED_FDS;
     struct rlimit limit;
 
